From fa1b6c5cfc59a7f4b6cdc28e1b9bb6f255354062 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:33:37 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(desktop): a remote's own update pauses SSH dials instead
 of redialing per request [daveey]

While hermes-hub ran its own `hermes update` (2026-10-27 20:58-21:00 PDT), every renderer request opened a
new SSH control master, hit the preflight's "SSH startup is paused" refusal and closed it: 53 dials in 108 s,
5 failed session opens. ensureRegistryBackend drops the pool entry on failure and nothing remembered the
refusal; only remote-prewarm had a backoff.

- remote-update-pause.ts: RemoteUpdatePause, keyed by remote install (user@host:port|hermes path). An
  'update-in-progress' refusal pauses the key; callers fail fast from the cached error (code
  remote-update-paused, retryAt) until the window (2/5/10/20/30 s) opens, then one caller probes; a success
  clears the pause and logs [remote-update-pause] ... reconnected once; any other failure ends the pause.
- main.ts: bootstrapSshConnection (every SSH dial) runs through the gate; managed update restores bypass it.
- remote-prewarm.ts: follow an error's retryAt instead of a second backoff, so prewarm is the probe that sees
  the update finish.
- remote-update-pause.test.ts: renderer-request and prewarm paths each prove at most one dial per backoff
  window during the update and a single reconnect after it; main.ts wiring pinned.

Includes fork follow-up 469b0731ea (test uses a documentation address).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
